I've left out the enrichment fetch. This node's `with` clause grants only `StateHandle<'seen', read-write>`, which is `aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0`. An outbound HTTP call would need `aap:caps/http-client` in the world, and that is authority the graph has not given this node. The approval has to show up as a change to the node's declared capabilities, such as `HTTPClient<[metadata.internal.example]>` in its `with` clause, not as an extra import added in the body. There is also a smaller problem: `published-digest` has no text field to put in `q=`. Once the graph declares the capability and says which field to send, I can add the fetch.

The node below only marks ids as seen. It writes to state only when the digest was actually published, so items from a failed publish are considered again on the next run.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        if published.published {
            let mut done: Vec<&str> = Vec::with_capacity(published.considered.len());
            for id in published.considered.iter() {
                let key = id.as_str();
                if key.is_empty() || done.contains(&key) {
                    continue;
                }
                state_rw::put(key, &published.date);
                done.push(key);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

